  "worked_solution": "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{\u2032}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(\\lambda )}{2!} & \u22ef & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{\u2032}(\\lambda ) & \u22ef & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ \u22ee & \u22f1 & \u22f1 & \u22f1 & \u22ee \\\\ 0 & \u22ef & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{\u2032}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & \u22ef & \u22ef & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)={3}^{x},$ $n=2,$ and $\\lambda =1.$\n\nSince $n=2,$ we need to compute the first $2-1=1$ derivatives of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}\u20613\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(1)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(1) & {f}^{\u2032}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{1} & {3}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}\u20613 \\\\ 0 & {3}^{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}\u20613 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
